// The `shell` format, v0. This file is NORMATIVE: the Rust parser in
// diet/src/formats/shell.rs implements it and is not itself the definition.
//
// A shell document is the command line one tool call handed to a shell. Two
// lanes read it and neither may guess at it:
//
//   * The mechanical lane derives the working directory from `cd`, `pushd`
//     and `popd`, and the files a turn touched from redirections and operand
//     words. The working directory was once an INTERVIEW QUESTION, and the
//     model got it wrong in exactly the way one would expect, because it was
//     reconstructing state it had no reason to track. A lane that derives it
//     from the command line is exact -- but only if the command line is read
//     as the shell reads it. `cd a; (cd b; ls); pwd` ends in `a`, and a reader
//     that does not know a subshell has its own working directory says `b`.
//   * The router classifies a call by what the model wanted from it, which is
//     the command that PRODUCES the line's output: the first command of the
//     last pipeline of the last item, descending into a subshell or group
//     that is itself the producer. `cd diet && cargo test` is a test run and
//     not a `cd`; `cargo test | tail -15` is a test run and not a `tail`;
//     `cat notes.md | head -20` is a read of `notes.md` and not a `head`.
//     Neither the first word of the line nor the last simple command written
//     answers this, so `List::producer` states the rule once and a router
//     that re-derives it is the second reader this format exists to prevent.
//
// So the shape of a command line is what this grammar states, and what it
// states is a SUBSET of the shell: lists, pipelines, simple commands with
// assignment prefixes and redirections, subshells and groups, the three
// quotings, expansions kept as opaque words, comments, line continuations and
// a heredoc when it ends its line. Control flow (`if`, `for`, `while`, `case`,
// functions) is NOT in this subset. Its keywords parse as ordinary words, so a
// loop body is seen as a sequence of simple commands -- which is right for
// "which files did this touch" and wrong for "how many times". A lane that
// needs the difference needs a later version of this file, not a tolerant
// reading of this one.
//
// What is deliberately refused, stated so nobody has to discover it: an
// unterminated quote, subshell, group or expansion; an operator with nothing
// after it (`a &&`); a redirection with no target (`echo >`); a heredoc that
// is not the last thing on its line (`cat <<EOF | sort` -- the body would have
// to be located past the rest of the line, and v0 does not); an empty
// subshell; a redirection or `|&` after a subshell or group; a redirection
// that hugs the word before it (`cmd>file`, `ls 2>&1>out`, `cmd&>/dev/null`);
// a `<<` terminator indented by tabs, which only `<<-` may be; arithmetic
// expansion (`$((1+2))`) and process substitution (`<(ls)`), each of which
// would need a form v0 does not have. Each is a fixture under
// `fixtures/invalid/` with its reason.
//
// The hugging redirection is the one refusal that costs something a shell
// would give: `cmd>file` is a command and a write, unambiguously, and v0 says
// no to it. The rule it protects is that a redirection is ITS OWN WORD, which
// is what lets `elements` separate on space alone and what keeps `echo 2 > f`
// (a word `2`) apart from `echo 2>f` (descriptor 2). Widening it would mean
// deciding, inside a rule that scans left to right, where a word ends and an
// operator begins -- and `&` is the case that shows the cost: `cmd&>/dev/null`
// is a redirection to the shell and `cmd& >/dev/null` is a background marker,
// and the two differ by one space. Refusing both is a subset. Reading one as
// the other is a lie about what ran.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// document
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// A document may be empty or all blank lines: a tool call with no command is a
// fact about the call, and the projection says so with an empty list.
document = { SOI ~ gap* ~ (list ~ gap*)? ~ EOI }

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// lists
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// `a; b`, `a && b || c`, `a &`, one per line. A separator may trail; a
// separator may not lead, and two may not meet -- `;;` belongs to `case`,
// which is not here.
list = { and_or ~ (sep ~ gap* ~ and_or)* ~ sep? }

// `&` sends the AND-OR list before it to the background. It is captured as
// its own rule so the parser can say which list it applied to; `;` and a
// newline are the plain sequence separators. A comment may sit in front of a
// newline separator and nowhere else.
sep = { hs* ~ (background | (";" ~ !";") | comment? ~ nl) }
background = { "&" ~ !"&" ~ !">" }

// `a && b || c` groups left to right, as the shell does. A newline may follow
// the operator.
and_or = { pipeline ~ (hs* ~ and_or_op ~ gap* ~ pipeline)* }
and_or_op = { and | or }
and = { "&&" }
or = { "||" }

// `a | b`, `a |& b`. A single `|` followed by another is `||` and belongs to
// the rule above; the lookahead keeps this one from taking half of it.
pipeline = { command ~ (hs* ~ pipe ~ gap* ~ command)* }
pipe = { "|&" | ("|" ~ !"|") }

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// commands
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Ordered: a subshell and a group each open with a character that is also
// legal inside a word, so they are tried first. A group's brace must be
// followed by whitespace -- `{a,b}` is a word, `{ a; }` is a group.
command = { subshell | group | simple }

// A subshell runs its list against a COPY of the shell's state. That is the
// entire reason it is a separate rule rather than a word: the mechanical lane
// discards what happened inside.
subshell = { "(" ~ gap* ~ list ~ gap* ~ close_paren }
close_paren = { ")" }

// A group runs its list against the shell's own state. The closing brace
// must be its own word, which the list's trailing separator or a newline
// provides.
//
// The closers are named rules so that a bracket left open is refused for
// its own reason. Written as bare literals, an unterminated subshell, an
// unterminated group and a dangling `&&` all failed with the same message
// at the same place -- "expected command" at the end of input -- and the
// conformance corpus could not tell the three refusals apart.
//
// A redirection after a compound command (`(ls) > out`) and `|&` after one
// are outside v0: the redirection would need a place on the compound
// command to live, and v0 gives it none rather than a wrong one.
group = { "{" ~ hs+ ~ gap* ~ list ~ gap* ~ close_brace }
close_brace = { "}" }

// Assignment prefixes come first and only first: `make key=value` is a
// command with an operand, `key=value make` is an assignment. A command may
// be assignments alone, or elements alone, and never nothing: a rule that
// accepted the empty string here would make `; ls` a list of two commands,
// the first of them empty, and an empty command is what a leading separator
// or a dangling `&&` leaves behind. Both are refused instead.
simple = { (assignment_prefix+ ~ elements?) | elements }
assignment_prefix = _{ assignment ~ (hs+ | &sep_ahead | &EOI) }
elements = _{ !lone_open_brace ~ !lone_close_brace ~ element ~ (hs+ ~ element)* }
sep_ahead = _{ ";" | "&" | "|" | ")" | "}" | nl | "#" }

// A lone `{` where a command should start is a group that failed to be one
// -- `{ ls` with no closing brace -- and not a command called `{`. Only at
// command position: `echo { a` is three words to the shell and to this rule.
lone_open_brace = _{ "{" ~ &(hs | nl | EOI) }

element = { redirection | word }

assignment = { name ~ "=" ~ word? }
name = @{ (ASCII_ALPHA | "_") ~ (ASCII_ALPHANUMERIC | "_")* }

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// redirections
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// `> f`, `>>f`, `2>&1`, `&> f`, `< f`, `<<< word`, and a heredoc. A file
// descriptor hugs its operator: `echo 2 > f` is a word `2` and a redirection
// of descriptor 1. A target is a word; a duplication's target is a descriptor
// or `-`.
redirection = { heredoc | dup | file_redirection }

file_redirection = { fd? ~ file_op ~ hs* ~ word }
file_op = { ">>" | "&>>" | "&>" | ">|" | ">" | "<<<" | "<" }

dup = { fd? ~ dup_op ~ hs* ~ dup_target }
dup_op = { ">&" | "<&" }
dup_target = @{ ASCII_DIGIT+ | "-" }

fd = @{ ASCII_DIGIT+ }

// A heredoc: the operator, its delimiter, then -- after the rest of the
// command line, which must be empty -- the body up to a line that is exactly
// the delimiter. The delimiter is pushed onto pest's stack when it is read and
// popped when the terminator line is matched, so the terminator is compared
// against the text that opened it. A quoted delimiter is pushed without its
// quotes, because that is what the shell compares against.
//
// `<<` and `<<-` are TWO RULES because their terminators differ. `<<-` strips
// leading tabs from every body line and from the terminator line, so a heredoc
// written inside an indented block reads as the shell reads it; `<<` strips
// nothing and its terminator must start the line. One rule with a tab-tolerant
// terminator would have made `<<` accept an indented one, which the shell does
// not -- and one rule with a strict terminator, which is what v0 shipped
// first, made `<<-` a `<<` under another name: the operator was recorded, the
// stripping was not done, and the tab-indented terminator every real `<<-` has
// was refused. An operator whose spelling is the only thing about it that is
// implemented is worse than one that is absent.
heredoc = _{ heredoc_strip | heredoc_plain }

heredoc_plain = { heredoc_op ~ heredoc_head ~ heredoc_body ~ heredoc_end }
heredoc_op = { "<<" ~ !"-" }
heredoc_body = @{ (!(PEEK ~ (nl | EOI)) ~ (!nl ~ ANY)* ~ nl)* }
// Named, not silent: a heredoc whose delimiter never arrives is then
// refused as "expected heredoc_end" at the end of the input, rather than
// as a failed `<` redirection back where the heredoc began.
heredoc_end = { POP ~ &(nl | EOI) }

heredoc_strip = { strip_op ~ heredoc_head ~ strip_body ~ strip_end }
strip_op = { "<<-" }
strip_body = @{ (!(tabs ~ PEEK ~ (nl | EOI)) ~ (!nl ~ ANY)* ~ nl)* }
strip_end = { tabs ~ POP ~ &(nl | EOI) }
// Tabs and only tabs: the shell strips tabs here and not spaces, and a rule
// that took `hs*` would have accepted a space-indented terminator the shell
// would have read as body.
tabs = _{ "\t"* }

heredoc_head = _{ hs* ~ heredoc_delim ~ hs* ~ comment? ~ nl }
heredoc_delim = _{ ("'" ~ PUSH(delim_text) ~ "'") | ("\"" ~ PUSH(delim_text) ~ "\"") | PUSH(delim_text) }
delim_text = @{ (ASCII_ALPHANUMERIC | "_" | "-" | ".")+ }

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// words
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// A word is one or more parts with nothing between them: `a'b c'd` is one
// word, `a 'b c' d` is three. A word may not begin a comment.
//
// A lone closing brace is refused at COMMAND POSITION only, by `elements`,
// and this rule is the test it uses. That is the mirror of `lone_open_brace`,
// and the two were once asymmetric: `{` was refused only where a command
// starts and `}` was refused wherever a word could sit, so `{ a` parsed and
// `echo }` did not, although the shell reads both as words. `}` still closes
// a group, because a group's body is a list and a list's commands all start
// at command position, which is exactly where the refusal now sits.
word = ${ !comment_start ~ word_part+ }
comment_start = _{ "#" }
lone_close_brace = _{ "}" ~ &(hs | nl | ";" | "&" | "|" | ")" | EOI) }

word_part = _{ single_quoted | double_quoted | expansion | escaped | bare }

// Single quotes keep every byte as written; nothing escapes inside them.
single_quoted = { "'" ~ single_inner ~ "'" }
single_inner = @{ (!"'" ~ ANY)* }

// Double quotes keep bytes as written except for a backslash before one of
// the five characters it can escape, and they may contain an expansion,
// which may itself contain a double quote.
double_quoted = { "\"" ~ double_part* ~ "\"" }
double_part = _{ double_escape | expansion | double_run | double_backslash }
double_escape = { "\\" ~ ("\"" | "\\" | "$" | "`" | nl) }
double_run = @{ (!("\"" | "\\" | "$" | "`") ~ ANY)+ }
// A backslash before any other character stays a backslash, as the shell
// keeps it: `"\n"` is two characters inside double quotes.
double_backslash = { "\\" }

// Expansions are kept opaque. What they expand to depends on state the
// grammar does not have, and a lane that needs the value marks the word as
// not literal rather than guessing.
expansion = { dollar_paren | dollar_brace | dollar_name | backtick | dollar_alone }
dollar_paren = { "$(" ~ paren_inner* ~ ")" }
paren_inner = _{ dollar_paren | single_quoted | double_quoted | escaped | (!")" ~ ANY) }
dollar_brace = { "${" ~ brace_inner* ~ "}" }
brace_inner = _{ dollar_brace | single_quoted | double_quoted | escaped | (!"}" ~ ANY) }
dollar_name = { "$" ~ (name | ASCII_DIGIT | "?" | "$" | "!" | "#" | "@" | "*") }
backtick = { "`" ~ (!"`" ~ ANY)* ~ "`" }
dollar_alone = { "$" }

// A backslash before a newline joins the lines; before anything else it keeps
// that character literally.
escaped = { "\\" ~ (nl | ANY) }

// Everything else, up to a character that means something to the grammar.
bare = @{ bare_char+ }
bare_char = _{ !(hs | nl | "'" | "\"" | "\\" | "$" | "`" | "|" | "&" | ";" | "(" | ")" | "<" | ">") ~ ANY }

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// whitespace and comments
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// `hs` is horizontal space; a backslash-newline is horizontal space too,
// because the shell splices the lines before it reads them.
//
// A carriage return is NOT horizontal space. It was, so that a CRLF file
// parsed -- and that made `echo a<CR>b` three words where the shell gives two,
// a wrong read rather than a refusal, on the one input class (a file written
// on Windows) the allowance existed to serve. The return belongs to the line
// ending instead: `nl` is CRLF or LF, and a return anywhere else is an
// ordinary character of the word it sits in, which is what the shell makes of
// it. This also makes `\<CRLF>` a line continuation, which the old pair read
// as an escaped return followed by a newline that ended the command.
hs = _{ " " | "\t" | ("\\" ~ nl) }
nl = _{ "\r"? ~ "\n" }

// A gap is anything that may sit between two items of a list: blank lines,
// comments, space.
gap = _{ hs | nl | comment }
// Silent: a comment is nothing to any reader, and a token for it would have
// to be skipped at every place a gap may sit.
comment = _{ "#" ~ (!nl ~ ANY)* }
